A||N||48||TwisT||Getting ready to install a "cold-air kit,"  need some advice!||||12||
Z||000000||TwisT||02-25-2001||12:32 PM||||I'm getting pretty pumped up about my project!  Today I was taking a look at my disassembled GT,  when I realized that this would be a GREAT time to install a "cold-air kit."  The previous owner had stupidly removed the stock air-intake tube just below the air filter,  allowing for hot air from the engine compartment to be sucked in.  NOT GOOD!  I think he was going for better flow (K&N filter also) but he failed to remember that hot air = less power.<P>I remember that Jonathan McCreery is the one to talk to on this,  but I also want to get some input from the forum  (Jonathan,  jump right into this).  If I could get some pictures of the install,  that would be great.  Also,  if anyone has their own way of doing it,  let me know.<P>If there are previous threads that I've missed on this subject,  please let me know!<P>Thanks Fiero gang!<P>Brett<BR><p>[This message has been edited by TwisT (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000001||TwisT||02-25-2001||06:16 PM||||*BUMP*||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||02-25-2001||06:34 PM||||IF you need help let me know , i will be doing the cold air thing to lisa's car this week.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||02-25-2001||07:07 PM||||May as well explain it all.....<P><BR>1st ..is it worth it??? Good question , most people have found they lost as bit of TQ , I have only ever seen 2 actual people put their cars on the dyno. My friend Mark waters was the second car I put a cold air kit onto. He has a 2.8 , ADS chip , MSD box , really clean car , well thought out. He gained 6Hp @ the wheels , but lost 7ft lbs tq. The result basically was a motor that was a bit more happy to rev, fiero's seem to have a Hump in the powerband.<P>Either way it's a cheap mod , The car makes really nifty induction sounds (haha) and for my purposes it helped , I autocross a lot.<P>Basically what you need to do:<P>Jack up the car , remove the drivers side rear wheel. Remove the inner fender liner.<P>there is a black air inlet box , it connects to the outside air inlet vent via a little rubber gasket. Keep everything you never know when someone may need it.<P>The air box is hard to get out , there is a nut up high that will take some work. Once you get the air box out , the hard part is over. Sometimes you have to break to box to get it out. My car had 20,000 original miles on it when I did the air induction and the bolt was rusted...It was very difficult to get it out. Also you need to remove all related ain intake items.. Air canister , everything! , when your done you will just have a hole in the rear inner fender where your canister sat.<P><BR>Now what you need to get is a chrome "Boom Tube" exhaust tip , these can be found at autozone , for canadians Crappy tire has these. The boom tube you want is 18" long with a 3" ID (inside diameter) , Now this will have a reduced end on it , and a slash cut end. Cut these off so it is a chrome cylinder roughly 11-13" long depending on the K&N filter you get.<P>The filter should be a K&N conical filter , this is the beauty of the system , the filter as we all know from their ads is washable and lasts forever. <P>Tip: Washing a K&N ..don't waste money on K&N's cleaner system , get the filter oil and wash it in the dishwasher. I thought this was a goofy idea until I saw a Ferrari cup car mechanic do it..case closed.<P><BR>Ok Now you have all original air intake parts out.  You need the original sensor that is in the air canister. Drill a hole in your chrome pipe and have a nut , or exhaust bing welded in so that the sensor can be threaded in easily. Don't have access to a welder...just thread it into the pipe hole..<P><BR>Now when you hook this all up your going to use the original Throttle body rubber elbow ..this is the one that comes off the TB and goes into the canister. The nice part is that it keeps everything lookin clean , not a hack job. The Elbow should be off of the TB.<P>You need to attach the filter to the fender and feed the chrome tube up through the hole in the inner fender.. This is best done as a 2 person job , someone needs to hold the chrome tube up top so that it doesn't fall back down into the fender. So with that done attach the chrome tube to the rubber elbow.<P>MAKE SURE TO TEST FIT THE ELBOW AND RUBBER TUBE!<P>It may take a little finessing... Everytime I do this it seems to vary based on milage of the car, ie rubber shrinks with heat.<P>This is easy use a dremel drill to take a bit of the rubber on the inside of the elbow out.. a shot of liquid wrench.. it should slide on snugly ..be patient!.<P>Now you have the filter , connected to the chrome tube , connected to the rubber elbow. Now it's time to connect it to the TB. , If you take your time it will connect up to the TB with little or no trouble , the metal line will connect back up to the Rubber TB elbow.<P>Re-connect sensor and your done  almost.<P>If you did the above right the filter will be viewable through the side vent. The problem is that you need to build a splash shield for the filter or else the filter gets extremely dirty. I made mine out of a rubber plumbing mat and sealed it with caulking... air tight<P>IF it won't reconnect to the TB easily... shorten the Chrome tube a bit ..it will fit , again be patient. The result is a clean looking install that most pople won't notice. I find the conversion useful because on the track I spend 90% of my time between 4500 and 6300rpm<P>I think thats about it.  If anyone has any questions let me know<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||02-25-2001||07:09 PM||||If your going to post how herb adams did this and didn't find any power..don't bother. Herb did it 15 years ago , The dyno session I was at was 2 years ago<P><BR>JM||||reg||1
Z||000005||TwisT||02-25-2001||07:27 PM||||Jonathan,  THANKS FOR THE VALUABLE INFO!<P>I'm doing this now,  beings the panels are stripped off the car and the air-box is easy to get at.  I'm going to give this a shot soon...  I'll post pictures when I complete the install.<P>The one question I have is the splash-guard.  Where abouts do you attach it and how?  I'm thinking it's put around the filter itself.  Am I right?  =)<P>Can't wait to hear that intake suckin'!<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||02-25-2001||07:48 PM||||Just a note, a noisy intake means the air entering it is turbulent and not flowing smoothly/freely enough. But, I hear it sounds AWESOME.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||02-25-2001||08:51 PM||||Not really, noisey air intake means there is no source of muffling in the air intake, The factory goes to great lengths to get rid of induction noise. If you take a look at the V6 fiero air box you can see they went to great lengths to make the car quieter. The box has a tiny inlet , leading into a lage open box then to a small outlet. <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||MikeSchaefer||02-25-2001||11:33 PM||||Slammed, pictures?  Sounds like you have the king of all cold air setups.  I would love to see it in place.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||02-25-2001||11:37 PM||||I will take pics this week when i do the installin lisa's car.. that way I can give a part # for the Filter and exact dimesnsions for the length of the tube..<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000010||TwisT||02-25-2001||11:48 PM||||Jonathan, sounds awesome...  can't wait.  Oh,  and by the way...  good timing!  =)  It's going to be a treat to see the master at work.<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||03-05-2001||12:12 AM||||Hey, Slammed, looking forward to some pics of the install!  Did you use the chrome tube mainly for looks?  It seems it would be much cheaper to just use 3" PVC pipe, or flexible dryer ducting.  Not as good looking, but just as functional, unless I'm missing something.  ||||reg||6
Z||000012||FieroLisa||03-05-2001||10:10 AM||||heres a pic of it installed....pics havent been posting well for me so i hope this works:<BR>finished product:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/fincoldair.jpg"> <BR>all the leftovers:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/leftovers.jpg"> <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A><P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>Tarheels Fieros Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||c m west||03-05-2001||12:53 PM|||| Here at West Cpast Fiero we offer a simple bolt in cold air intake system that replaces the restrictive factory system. It utilyzes all of the original equipment such as the Mass Air Temperature Sensor, as well as the breather tube from the front valve cover ...<BR>if any one is interested then check us out<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||beer_stud_21||03-05-2001||02:32 PM||||Well, i got one question about this.  I was planning on doing this myself, I have everything torn out.  My question is about the water..  I live in hawaii where we can get inches of rain a day (as many and 20+ some days)  If i do the cold air induction and route pipe from the side of the car without the air restrictor will I have to worry about getting water in my engine on rainy days?  I was planning on doing essentually the same thing slammed did.  My idea to get rid of the water was to use PVC or something simular and then drill lots of small holes on the downhill side of it so that most of the water would run out before it reached the air filter..  You guys think this would work?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Slammed Fiero||03-05-2001||03:13 PM||||No , below the filter you have a small splash gaurd , it keeps road debris from getting onto the filter , I have driven with mine in for quite some time now , running track events in the rain , no problem. All in all Lisa's system cost $26.00 for the pipe and $40 for the filter , I believe it was part # 3530 ,  so for $66 you have the complete system , looks great uses the factory air sensor , chrome pipe. and foremost is cheap , I have seen guys copy my system and want $130- $200 for it. Just nuts , I sold them a while back for $80 for the whole thing. Some people use PVC piping and what not as a cheap alternative to the chrome<P>Anyone has any questions feel free to post as I have installed quite a few of these on peoples cars.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Slammed Fiero||03-05-2001||03:17 PM||||I was in a bind when I did lisa's as we didn't have a factory wheel lock , so I did it with the wheel on the car , But I did jack up the car. the hardest part is getting the air box out. I think it took  me around 2 hours to do it all , would have been a lot less if I could have gotten the wheel off.<P>Lisa noticed a big difference in upper end power after we installed the system as well...<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000017||COUNTIBLIS||03-05-2001||05:01 PM||||Are the pics ready yet?  I want to hear that sound also, any audio?  <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/count3.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||FieroLisa||03-05-2001||05:05 PM||||definitely noticed the difference, it rocked my world   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>BTW are those pics that I posted above showing up for everyone, because they aren't on my computer.<P>If by chance you can't see the pics, the 2 I posted and another one is at <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/86GT.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/86GT.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by FieroLisa (edited 03-05-2001).]||||reg||1
