Z||000020||COUNTIBLIS||03-05-2001||05:07 PM||||no they arent<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/count3.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Jaei Sun||03-05-2001||06:01 PM||||I believe my cold intake has a few advantages over the method discussed.<P>I am using a Ractive Conical intake ($35!) that has a heat shield built over it. It sits inside the engine bay where the canister with the filter used to be. The tip (the only open part) of the filter is inside the hole where the filter is run in the described setup. So it still sucks in cold air outta the "dead space" compartment.<P>Advantage: I don't have to jack my car up, remove the wheel, remove the well to get the filter off and clean it.<P>And since it is shielded inside that compartment, I don't have to worry about water from the engine bay vent getting on my air filter element.<P>I might be changin my set up when an engine swap takes place but till then I'm happy with this.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaei Sun (edited 03-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||TwisT||03-05-2001||08:38 PM||||Good to see so much discussion on this subject!  I've been working too much in the last couple of days to really stay on top of this.  It's GREAT to see so many people sharing their ideas and versions.  <P>I have not yet done this mod...  sudden unforseen debts have left me broke,  if not broken.  Hopefully soon,  though...<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000023||DXR_DAD||03-05-2001||09:12 PM||||Jaei Sun, Do you have pics of your set-up?<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||5
Z||000024||Jaei Sun||03-05-2001||09:53 PM||||I don't have any yet.  But I will be getting ahold of the digital camera I use to take fiero pix pretty soon.  I'll post them as soon as I get them.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Slammed Fiero||03-05-2001||09:53 PM||||I wish i could post a picture!!!!<P>First , I have a splash shield on the under side of the car , it is glued in place then caulked to prevent water from entering in... I don't see how water could even touch the filter really , the base of the filter is rubber , I have never had any problem. For the extra $5 over the reactive filter I would rather get the K&N for reputation and it's ability to be cleaned. <P><BR>""And it sits inside the engine bay where the canister with the filter used to be was""<P>This is my main problem , your filter is sitting where the old one basically used to be ..ie hot engine air enters in. Mine is in the inside of the fender , you pull off that little side engine vent on the body and the filter is right their. Being in the side of the fender it basically creates a big air box.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/stlac2001/cold.jpg"> <P>If that doesn't work , just go to the link , I drew a diagram , hope it helps to explain how this all works. I think with the heat of the engine , espically a fiero the further away the filter the better. I have run with slicks on that throw up more dirt than you can well imagine, I have driven the car all over  Canada and the US , even to my cottage , a 1/4 hour trip down a dirt road... 3 years later Ihave had to clean the filter. Want too see if the filter is dirty?? pull off the body side vent and put your hand in. <P>It's simple , it's cheap and it works...<P><BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000026||DXR_DAD||03-05-2001||11:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B> Here at West Cpast Fiero we offer a simple bolt in cold air intake system that replaces the restrictive factory system. It utilyzes all of the original equipment such as the Mass Air Temperature Sensor, as well as the breather tube from the front valve cover ...<BR>if any one is interested then check us out<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Eric does this filter also sit inside the fender? seems to have a very long tube??<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com/Intake/Intake_Air_Tube.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cwestco.com/Intake/Intake_Air_Tube.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000027||c m west||03-07-2001||12:07 PM||||this sits just inside the fender - out of the HOT engine bay - with a conacal elemnet filter that sits just 1/4" behind the intake screen on the driver side of the car. The advantage of this setup is that with the tube lenth it tends to straighten out the air flow before being sucked into the motor, thereby decreasing turbulence and adding a slight gain in horsepower. The problem with a setup that sits inside the engine bay is that it will just be sucking up all the hot air from within - remeber to truly be a cold air intake the air charge must be significantly lower in temberature to that which is in the engine bay - colder air = more dense = more horsepower....<P>besides, we have them built, ready to simply bolt in with a filter, and you dont have to rig up something yourself to try to make it work - ours is proven because I have done all the R&D to get it JUST RIGHT - all you have to do is order it, and bolt it in....<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||lou_dias||03-07-2001||12:16 PM||||What I am looking for is something that bolts into the canister that houses the factory air filter and eliminates the air silencer below that and can be bonded into my Mecham side scoops...  Anybody have that?||||reg||1
Z||000029||c m west||03-07-2001||12:29 PM||||we offer that as well, it ios called the Velocity Tube - it goes for $45 and is made from 3" T-6 aluminum tubing and comes satin finished. This is a moderate modification to make to the car as opposed to the Cold Air intake that we offer, it cannot be seen from the engine bay ( big concern for Judges at Fiero Shows ) and gets rid of the restrictive air box..<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>Eric - West Coast Fiero<P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Shaun41178||03-07-2001||01:32 PM||||I think $145 for a cold air kit from WEst Coast is a good deal.  it was $250.  Compare it with A Fierot Store Cold air unit for like $275 or something wild like that.<P>I made my own cold air with a K&N filter and some 3 inch duct tubing.  If the price was $145 when I went to make mine I would have said screw it and bought the West Coast unit.  It looks good and is inline with all the prices for those import intakes so on't think anyone should complain that the price is too high.  Sure you can do the work yourself which to be honest isn't all that hard but does take a few hours and some driving to get the parts you need.  Plus the drilling and stuff to mount the pcv tube and MAt sensor.  West Coast already has it done.  They have the best setup for a Fiero that I have seen yet.<P>No I don't work for West coast or affiliated with them as you can see I live in toledo.  Well used to cause now I live in Daytona Beach.  Just trying to lend a little support to some Fiero vendors that offer a good product like this intake.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000031||FieroLisa||03-07-2001||02:00 PM||||I paid right around $70 for mine, with a chrome pipe...looks and sounds damn nice.||||reg||1
Z||000032||c m west||03-07-2001||02:55 PM||||I just want to make sure that no one is getting confused.. we offer 2 intake items thus far.....<P>Velocity Tube - 3" T-6 aluminum tube that replaces the stock air box just below the filter canister, produces a throatier sound from the motor while creating less turbulence, $45 with a satin finish, ready to bolt in<P>Cold Air Intake - completely replaces ALL of the factory intake hardware, extremely effiecint compared to the factory set up. This tube has a conical filter elemnt that site just behind the intake vent on the driver's side and creates a nearly straight path to the intake manifold, less turbulence=more horsepower. Also proivides a great sound from the motor while decreasing intake temperatures and rasing horsepower gains, I can have it painted any color you guys want and do my best to have a matching color filter, or I can send it out raw steel for powder coating or so that you can paoint it - $175 with filter, simply bolt it in - accepts MAT sensor and valve cover breather tube.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||Shaun41178||03-07-2001||03:50 PM||||oops sorry i meant $175 not $145.  mistype on my part.  Still a good deal though.||||reg||1
Z||000034||TwisT||03-07-2001||06:43 PM||||A question here for anyone...<P>Would it be worth wrapping the air-intake tube (that which is exposed in the engine bay) with some sort of insulation?  I'm thinking of material like which is used on headers,  or a similar product.  It gets hot as hell under the hood,  but I don't know if wrapping the intake would be worth the trouble or would be a benefit. <P>Any thoughts?<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000035||DXR_DAD||03-07-2001||08:20 PM||||The Mustang style scoop would be a better bet as far as getting some of the heat out of the engine bay?? I'm sure a combination<BR>of all mods (engine cooling, cold air intake, side scoops) would be beneficial?<BR>How much? That is the question.<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000036||lowCG||03-07-2001||08:28 PM||||I had to put a water shunt just inside the side scoop/fender,made from a heat-formed piece of ABS plastic,where I've mounted an original sized K&N,capped off with some Home Depot sewer parts.Also put another splash shield underneath,on the subframe to keep water from the rear tire from soiling my filter setup.It was made from some steel decking with lots of tiny holes.So far so good.||||reg||1
Z||000037||c m west||03-07-2001||09:04 PM||||like I said, I R&D'd the tube on my car, and by permission of customers cars, this tube that we make has been on the market for a little over a year now with absolutley no complaints. There is no cause to be alarmed about getting water into it - unless you drive into a pond - water will not be taken in with the intake charge just through shear design, so there is no fear of hydrolock<P>..... as far as Mustang stuff goes, I was raised with a bunch of friends that all have Mustangs, you must mean the March system that looks like a dust-buster under neath the radiator, the problem with this is that there is a high pressure area under the car at high speed, thereby creating distorted air flow for any mechanism to bring in the intake charge- not only that but you risk sucking in road debris into your motor...<P>all I am trying to say is that we have gone to the trouble to design an efficient system for you guys that is actually cost effective and very efficient.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
